Most definitely! Anything in particular you had in mind? The biggest interesting
'discovery' I made recently involves how to handle two very similar message
triggers with distinctly different sounds.

example: a) *shatter* b) *statue shatters*

We obviously don't want the same sound to play for both,  so we create a sound
for each message, not just one for 'shatter'. The trick is to put the *statue
shatters* first so that if that message ever comes up it takes priority over all
others that share a trigger!

Another example that was even more agitating:

ex. a) *hits* b) *boulder hits you*

Again, it's handy to have a blanket message for when something hits something
else, but what if a boulder hits you? That sounds odd when it makes the same
noise as a monster hitting you. Well, use the priority solution with specific
messages added and viola! Problem solved!

This is pretty exciting stuff! What this means is that if someone really really
really had too much free time, _every_ _single_ _message_ that could _ever_
appear in the game could have a specific sound. Obviously this isn't desirable
and some messages would never have sound, but at least it's possible to have
huge amounts of control over what sounds play when.

I don't have the sounds for it but my friend and I always joke about having
different sounds for every monster! =P

